  {
    "equipment_id": "1633",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1633",
    "equipment_name": "TruLaser 2030 (WC 2001)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "19067",
    "date_notified": "04/13/2026",
    "urgency": "3. Prod Affected, Not Stopped",
    "problem": "were getting a fork frame light sensor error",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "1.5",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "04/13/2026 09:00 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 04/13/26 10:18:55 AM: This has been giving them issues quite a bit recently. The front left sensor on the fork frame is the one giving issues. When it's extended it isn't straight at all. I removed the sensor bracket from the cylinder. It is straight and doesn't have any issues. The cylinder is missing one rod. I turned the machine air off and removed the cylinder. Took it back to maintenance to see if I could repair it. We had a good used one, and two brand new ones. I removed the air fittings and installed them on the used cylinder. Took to the machine and mounted back on. Mounted sensor and bracket back on the cylinder. Plugged air lines in and turned air on. Had Vern run the machine. No issues with this anymore. Cylinder is moving freely as it should up and down. Machine isn't throwing any errors.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "1633",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1633",
    "equipment_name": "TruLaser 2030 (WC 2001)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "19630",
    "date_notified": "05/29/2026",
    "urgency": "6. General Maint",
    "problem": "The new 2030 laser, the cage on the south side needs to be extended to reach past the light curtain. (Preston is aware)",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "4",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "06/01/2026 11:00 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 06/01/26  7:32:38 AM: Got 2 cage panels at 58\". Found 2 posts out back that were on another panel. They are the only 2 I could find for this style 7' safety cage. I removed them from the panel and got them setup with the existing cage wall. Mounted together and anchored all down. Straightened the cage wall so it was in alignment. Mounted 2 small guards on the north side of the machine as well. Cut off the tabs on them so they would fit the machine better. Adjusted outside cage bolts on the north side. Smoot carriage head was on the inside, not on the outside as it should be. Machine is ready to run.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "1633",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1633",
    "equipment_name": "TruLaser 2030 (WC 2001)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "19658",
    "date_notified": "06/02/2026",
    "urgency": "1. Prod Stopped",
    "problem": "The 2030 fiber laser is throwing an error on the dust collector (Preston is aware)",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "150",
    "labor_time": "4",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "06/03/2026 01:30 PM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 06/05/26  5:17:01 AM: There was a circuit breaker that was tripping in the dust collector electrical cabinet. The circuit breaker was tripping because the contactor that it runs off of was not de-energizing as it should. It was staying locked in causing an amperage spike. I removed the contactor and brought to maintenance. Took the contactor all the way apart. One of the phase contact points was broken completely inside. This was leaving at least one phase latched in when the contactor is supposed to be de-energized. It is not repairable. I found 5 in stock on Mouser. Everywhere else was 4+week leadtime from the manufacturer. I sent to Paul and he went ahead and ordered all 5 of them. They arrived 6/3 before 10am. I put the new contactor in and got all wires tied back in. Unlocked the machine and turned on. Machine started up with no issues and is running good. Hours later they were having an issues where it was saying low air pressure. The main incoming wasn't low. I made sure nothing was tripped and all was good. I checked the regulator inside the machine and it was reading above 6 bar. I adjusted the regulator up to 6.5 bar. Machine is running good like this. Not sure if we have a switch going bad, or if we're still fine tuning the way it runs here. I am monitoring. They ran all day yesterday with no issues.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "1633",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1633",
    "equipment_name": "TruLaser 2030 (WC 2001)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "19889",
    "date_notified": "06/18/2026",
    "urgency": "1. Prod Stopped",
    "problem": "the suction cups will not kick on air.",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "8",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "06/22/2026 07:00 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 06/23/26  5:47:40 AM: We came in 6/18 and Vern was having issues with the machine. Suction frame is functioning as it should. The vacuum isn't engaging like it should. It is taking too long and showing this error. Suction frame vacuum error while indexed to the machine. Created a case and got with tech support. Made sure there was nothing in the way to keep it from functioning. Checked suction cups for any issues. Michael@Trumpf got with me. He said to clean them if dirty, check for air leaks, sheet size, etc. All are good. Had me check solenoid connector 11 and 12. 12 is active when vacuum is off. When engaged, valve shifts and engages 11. That sends air the the valve selector. All of that is working as it should. When the valve selector lights are on, that means there is no pressure going out of that port. When the lights are off, that means there should be air pressure coming out of that port. All of this is working as it should. Went back and forth with Michael trying to determine the issue. When we came in 6/22, we changed material and attempted running another job. Ran this with no problem. Machine ran all day with no issues. Not sure what the issue was. Michael said normally this is a mechanical issue, not electrical. I will monitor, but machine is running great.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "1633",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1633",
