    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "07/30/2026 09:00 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 08/07/26  7:48:53 AM: Checked control cabinet for cooling unit. The static pressure switch was tripped. This caused a high pressure read. I had Vern cut new filters for the cooling unit cabinet. While he was doing that, I blew it out with air. It was really dirty inside. Got done cleaning, replacing the filters, and resetting the switch. Cycled the machine power. No more errors and it is running with no issues.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "1633",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 1633",
    "equipment_name": "TruLaser 2030 (WC 2001)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "20465",
    "date_notified": "08/24/2026",
    "urgency": "1. Prod Stopped",
    "problem": "Water leak above cutting head (Preston is aware)",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "2.75",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "08/24/2026 08:30 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 08/24/26 10:26:13 AM: Machine had a water leak Saturday. Got with Matthew John first thing. I removed the tracking as needed to find the leak. We had to turn the machine on and run for a bit to get the water pressure back up. Found the leak. It was on a black 10mm festo line that went from the top of the machine through the tracking down to the cutting head. It was only 6 feet or so. I removed the entire line and replaced it with a new one. Refit it through the tracking and got it all clamped back up. Put the tracking cover on. I left the main cover off so we could test the machine for leaks. I had to remove the safety switch from the machine cover and put it in place so the machine would run with the cover off. We ran multiple sheets with no issues. There isn't any water leaking from anywhere. I put on the last covers, and everything is good to run production.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "5340",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 5340",
    "equipment_name": "TruMatic 6000",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "12432",
    "date_notified": "07/17/2024",
    "urgency": "3. Prod Affected, Not Stopped",
    "problem": "grip master grippers hitting hard and the cam down pin rubbing down the side of the table (Preston aware)",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "4",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "07/17/2024 01:00 PM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 07/18/24  5:35:16 AM: We ran 3 different programs with no issues. During this, we had an issue opening the left door to the scrap area. I had to remove the bottom of the safety switch so we could open the door. The plastic roller inside was broken and wasn't fully disengaging the door switch. Paul is getting another one ordered. I removed the door switch plate and left it engaged in the safety switch. It is bypassed right now, but both doors will be open if the area needs entry, and the other safety switch is working. The chiller unit inside the machine behind the material table was low on water. Filled up with water from the forklift battery watering station. We got the missing cage in. I removed the wiring the techs temporarily installed and got the new gate/cage installed. Everything on the machine is good. I will swap out the sensor when it arrives. | Preston Ellexson - 07/18/24  4:49:59 AM: After working with tech support and adjusting the cylinder sensors related to this, I found the grip master anchors weren't tight anymore. Tried tightening the back left one towards the forklift aisle. It wasn't tight in the concrete anymore, and I pulled it out by hand. Had to drill a 3/4\" hole and cut rebar. Put in a longer anchor and it tightened up. The adjustable foot catty cornered to this was loose and had a gap allowing the table to be pushed down. Got the foot adjusted out tight. Tried to tightened the silver anchor and the anchor is just spinning with the nut. It's still tight but paritally not grabbing the concrete anymore. Once everything was tight, we are not hitting anymore. The brass roller has plenty of clearance from the table. Grip master fingers aren't hitting anything either.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "5340",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 5340",
