    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "07/09/2026 03:30 PM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 08/12/26 11:44:49 AM: Removed all hydraulic lines, cooler, motor wiring, and solenoid connectors. Got Jimmie to help on the forklift. Removed hydraulic/motor assembly from the machine and put on saw horses. Removed oil from machine and cleaned all particles of the spider out. Got the shaft couplers set where they need to be. I used permanent loctite on them. I used it last time as well, and it still came loose. Got all lines back together on the motor and pump. Put the assembly back on the machine. Got lined up and put a new gasket seal on the plate. Tightened all down and reconnected all lines and wiring. Filled tank with oil. Removed all repair parts and oil containers from the machine. Unlocked it, turned on, and tested. Machine is running with no issues. Ram and everything is working the way it should. I am still monitoring to see if our main board needs to be swapped out, or if the issues with that have resolved. | Preston Ellexson - 06/30/26  5:19:33 AM: Locked the machine out. Disconnect switch was loose from the inside of the cabinet. Got it tight and working good. Removed the bolts on the motor/pump assembly plate. Used a pry bar to raise up and block up so I could see the housing for the lovejoy coupler. Looks like the set screw on the pump shaft coupler loosened, and the coupler slowly dropped down the shaft. It dropped low enough to separate from the spider. At that point, the top of the pump coupler was scraping the bottom of the motor coupler. This will be a pretty easy fix, just time consuming. I'll have to drain the oil to remove any broken spider particles, etc. I'll have to remove the motor/pump assembly and take it out. I'll separate the pump lines and pump bolts enough to slide on the new coupler. I am working on this after I get the TruMatic 6000 back on line, or a tech arrives to complete.",
    "attachments": []
  },
