  {
    "equipment_id": "192",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 192",
    "equipment_name": "Press Brake #44 (WC 2018)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "17969",
    "date_notified": "12/16/2025",
    "urgency": "1. Prod Stopped",
    "problem": "machine is calibrating in reverse (Preston is aware)",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "1.5",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "12/17/2025 07:30 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 12/17/25 11:14:28 AM: Sent in a service request for a tech to reach out. Made a 20' ethernet cable. Loaded TeamViewer on the machine. Got everything ready in case Capital called and wanted to log in. Tech called right when I left work. He said to check the backgauge switch and make sure it's functioning correctly. The backgauge will come forward at a slower rate when it thinks it's already past it's home position. Came in first thing today and found the switch was stuck shut. Removed needed covers and removed the switch. Cleaned and it is still functioning and working correctly. Put covers back on and turned the machine on. Calibrated with no issues. Had Chad set the mute point on the light curtains and check program. After adjusting the slow on the program, machine is running great. Foot pedal is working good. Backgauge is functioning as it should. Machine is ready to run.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "192",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 192",
    "equipment_name": "Press Brake #44 (WC 2018)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "17853",
    "date_notified": "12/06/2025",
    "urgency": "6. General Maint",
    "problem": "the ground wire for the machine is out in the walkway too far and the operator is tripping over it when he has to go in and out to turn the machine on and off.",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "0.5",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "12/17/2025 08:30 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 12/17/25 11:18:36 AM: Drilled ground rod hole in concrete much closer to the machine. Used hammer drill to fully drive the ground rod all the way down. Cleaned burrs off of the top of the rod. Swapped ground clamp to new rod and drove the old one in below flush. Ground is good and tight. I tripped on the old ground while doing my last job, which led me to go ahead and knock this out too. Machine is good to run.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "192",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 192",
    "equipment_name": "Press Brake #44 (WC 2018)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "19599",
    "date_notified": "05/28/2026",
    "urgency": "1. Prod Stopped",
    "problem": "The 3013-2 pan die a part got stuck in it.",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "0.75",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "05/28/2026 09:30 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 05/28/26  8:01:20 AM: Pan either wasn't notched correctly, or was put in the die wrong. I believe it was a part issue and not the operator. Removed ram from dies and raised all the way up. Left the bottom dies tight in the bed rail. Removed the front plate on the forming die operation and separated from the dowel pins. Got the part removed from the die. Back side of the die plate has normal use wear. I stoned the back side to smooth out as much as possible. Put back on the die. Had operator reseat top dies and run a part to test. Part formed with no issues. Die is good to use.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "192",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 192",
    "equipment_name": "Press Brake #44 (WC 2018)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "19683",
    "date_notified": "06/03/2026",
    "urgency": "1. Prod Stopped",
    "problem": "Machine is throwing codes and wont run. (Preston is aware)",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "1",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "06/03/2026 03:00 PM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 06/04/26  7:58:16 AM: Jim just started having this issue today. Sometimes the ram will work, and randomly it won't. No pedestal error codes, aside from the intended 2 operator codes. LaserSafe hmi says no error for this. Checked the LaserSafe cabinet. When Jim is having issues, I'm getting a few codes I've never seen. 4154, 4145, 4305, and 43E1. Sent these to Loris@THCO for information. He let me know each individual code, but said it's all related to an alignment issue. Got Chad over after Jim left to check alignment. He realized the receiver was picking up the transmitter from BP#44. Merritt hasn't had issues, and didn't realize that his transmitter arm wasn't fully tight and was creeping down. We got that set back and the issues with Jim's machine went away. No more issues.",
    "attachments": []
  },
  {
    "equipment_id": "192",
    "equipment_eq_id": "EQ ID 192",
    "equipment_name": "Press Brake #44 (WC 2018)",
    "department": "Fabrication",
    "wo_id": "20333",
    "date_notified": "08/12/2026",
    "urgency": "3. Prod Affected, Not Stopped",
    "problem": "The double stop button on the pedestal will not turn off.",
    "status": "Closed and Completed",
    "material_cost": "0",
    "labor_time": "3",
    "work_performed_by": "Preston",
    "assigned_to": "Maintenance",
    "downtime_hours": "0",
    "completed_datetime": "09/08/2026 11:00 AM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 09/23/26  7:46:53 AM: Jim is doing training on Tuesdays. I scheduled this with Shacole. I locked the machine out. Removed the pedestal top and ran continuity tests between buttons. Found a string of buttons that were not working at all. Removed overlay and cleaned existing glue with acetone. Put a new overlay in place and plugged in. Checked everything and then removed lock and powered up the machine. Everything is working as it should now. The pedestal circuit board must have a small short in it somewhere. The ringer for the ram level adjustment is only supposed to ring when the ram level is being adjusted. It is staying on. Shut machine down and locked out. Replaced the pedestal circuit board and turned machine back on. No more ringing, and everything is working good. Released for production. | Preston Ellexson - 08/25/26  7:18:06 AM: Looking at this shortly. | Preston Ellexson - 08/19/26  8:25:11 AM: Shacole wants me to get this done on a Tuesday. Jim is in meetings then. While I fix this, I am going to raise the machine up like we originally planned. If BP#56 isn't back up and running by next Tuesday, I'll hold off on this repair. I believe this is a bad pedestal overlay. She said the ram level isn't working now either. I don't think the pedestal board would cause this. I am going to test the overlay first and go from there. | Preston Ellexson - 08/12/26 11:30:18 AM: Got with Shacole and Jim on this. It isn't causing Jim issues with the setup/part he's running. It will when he runs certain parts. She said he's good this week. When I get back Monday, I'll check this out. Might just be an overlay, but I want to be sure.",
    "attachments": []
  },
