    "completed_datetime": "02/06/2026 02:30 PM",
    "comments": "Preston Ellexson - 02/09/26  7:37:53 AM: Removed the flywheel cover. Everything looks good. Pulley is still tight. Shaft bushing for pulley is still tight too. Belts are all in good shape. Motor doesn't show any signs of wear or failure. Reversed phase rotation back and turned breaker on. Unlocked machine and started up. Had Ken operate the punch while I watched everything function. I don't see any issues at all. I believe when the machine bottomed out, it tried to slip the pulley. This is due to the flywheel weight and momentum. This caused the burning smell because the motor was still trying to move the flywheel but couldn't since the die was bottomed out. The motor is heavy duty and was purchased as an OEM replacement no too long ago, otherwise it might've taken the motor out. I greased the motor while I had the cover off. Everything looks good and is safe to run. I put the cover back on and the machine is ready for production. | Preston Ellexson - 02/06/26  6:17:36 AM: Checked this out when it happened. Something is creating a burning smell. The motor is not overheating. The pulley is overheating for some reason. Machine was on bottom. I reversed the rotation to see if it was locked up. It was not and reversed easily. I locked it out. I will need to remove the cover and inspect the motor/flywheel assembly. I plan on getting to this by the end of today.",
    "attachments": []
  },
